What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/disksize
Date:		August 2010
Contact:	Nitin Gupta <ngupta@vflare.org>
Description:
		The disksize file is read-write and specifies the disk size
		which represents the limit on the *uncompressed* worth of data
		that can be stored in this disk.
		Unit: bytes

What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/initstate
Date:		August 2010
Contact:	Nitin Gupta <ngupta@vflare.org>
Description:
		The initstate file is read-only and shows the initialization
		state of the device.

What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/reset
Date:		August 2010
Contact:	Nitin Gupta <ngupta@vflare.org>
Description:
		The reset file is write-only and allows resetting the
		device. The reset operation frees all the memory associated
		with this device.

What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/max_comp_streams
Date:		February 2014
Contact:	Sergey Senozhatsky <sergey.senozhatsky@gmail.com>
Description:
		The max_comp_streams file is read-write and specifies the
		number of backend's zcomp_strm compression streams (number of
		concurrent compress operations).

What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/comp_algorithm
Date:		February 2014
Contact:	Sergey Senozhatsky <sergey.senozhatsky@gmail.com>
Description:
		The comp_algorithm file is read-write and lets to show
		available and selected compression algorithms, change
		compression algorithm selection.

What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/mem_used_max
Date:		August 2014
Contact:	Minchan Kim <minchan@kernel.org>
Description:
		The mem_used_max file is write-only and is used to reset
		the counter of maximum memory zram have consumed to store
		compressed data. For resetting the value, you should write
		"0". Otherwise, you could see -EINVAL.
		Unit: bytes

What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/mem_limit
Date:		August 2014
Contact:	Minchan Kim <minchan@kernel.org>
Description:
		The mem_limit file is write-only and specifies the maximum
		amount of memory ZRAM can use to store the compressed data.
		The limit could be changed in run time and "0" means disable
		the limit. No limit is the initial state.  Unit: bytes

What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/compact
Date:		August 2015
Contact:	Minchan Kim <minchan@kernel.org>
Description:
		The compact file is write-only and trigger compaction for
		allocator zrm uses. The allocator moves some objects so that
		it could free fragment space.

What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/io_stat
Date:		August 2015
Contact:	Sergey Senozhatsky <sergey.senozhatsky@gmail.com>
Description:
		The io_stat file is read-only and accumulates device's I/O
		statistics not accounted by block layer. For example,
		failed_reads, failed_writes, etc. File format is similar to
		block layer statistics file format.

What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/mm_stat
Date:		August 2015
Contact:	Sergey Senozhatsky <sergey.senozhatsky@gmail.com>
Description:
		The mm_stat file is read-only and represents device's mm
		statistics (orig_data_size, compr_data_size, etc.) in a format
		similar to block layer statistics file format.

What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/debug_stat
Date:		July 2016
Contact:	Sergey Senozhatsky <sergey.senozhatsky@gmail.com>
Description:
		The debug_stat file is read-only and represents various
		device's debugging info useful for kernel developers. Its
		format is not documented intentionally and may change
		anytime without any notice.

What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/backing_dev
Date:		June 2017
Contact:	Minchan Kim <minchan@kernel.org>
Description:
		The backing_dev file is read-write and set up backing
		device for zram to write incompressible pages.
		For using, user should enable CONFIG_ZRAM_WRITEBACK.

What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/idle
Date:		November 2018
Contact:	Minchan Kim <minchan@kernel.org>
Description:
		idle file is write-only and mark zram slot as idle.
		If system has mounted debugfs, user can see which slots
		are idle via /sys/kernel/debug/zram/zram<id>/block_state

What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/writeback
Date:		November 2018
Contact:	Minchan Kim <minchan@kernel.org>
Description:
		The writeback file is write-only and trigger idle and/or
		huge page writeback to backing device.

What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/bd_stat
Date:		November 2018
Contact:	Minchan Kim <minchan@kernel.org>
Description:
		The bd_stat file is read-only and represents backing device's
		statistics (bd_count, bd_reads, bd_writes) in a format
		similar to block layer statistics file format.

What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/writeback_limit_enable
Date:		November 2018
Contact:	Minchan Kim <minchan@kernel.org>
Description:
		The writeback_limit_enable file is read-write and specifies
		eanbe of writeback_limit feature. "1" means eable the feature.
		No limit "0" is the initial state.

What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/writeback_limit
Date:		November 2018
Contact:	Minchan Kim <minchan@kernel.org>
Description:
		The writeback_limit file is read-write and specifies the maximum
		amount of writeback ZRAM can do. The limit could be changed
		in run time.

What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/low_compress_ratio
Date:		May 2025
Contact:	Fer1d
Description:
		The low_compress_ratio file is read-write and holds the
		threshold, in percent of the original page size, below which
		a compressed page is counted as poorly compressed and
		reported as the tenth field of mm_stat. The default is 75;
		setting it to 0 disables the accounting.

What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/idle_stat
Date:		May 2025
Contact:	Fer1d
Description:
		The idle_stat file is read-only and reports, for pages that
		survived 1..10 idle markings, how many pages fall into each
		generation. A page that is written again starts over.

What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/new_stat
Date:		May 2025
Contact:	Fer1d
Description:
		The new_stat file is read-only and reports the number of
		allocated pages that have not been marked idle yet.

What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/new
Date:		May 2025
Contact:	Fer1d
Description:
		The new file is write-only; writing "all" clears the idle
		state and the idle generation counter of every page.

What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/pool_policy
Date:		November 2026
Description:
		CONFIG_ZRAM_POOL_POLICY only, mode 0600. Read/write four decimal
		unsigned integers: enabled low_mib high_mib min_age_seconds.
		Default: 0 512 768 60. Require enabled in {0,1},
		0 < low_mib < high_mib <= UINT_MAX, positive age <= UINT_MAX.
		Updates are atomic and advance demand sequence, including rewrites.
		Thresholds measure physical zsmalloc allocated pages in MiB, not
		compressed data bytes, logical occupancy or disksize. Reset disables
		policy but preserves configured thresholds and age. Build/runtime
		defaults disabled. No debugfs dependency, no automatic BIO submission.

What:		/sys/block/zram<id>/pool_demand
Date:		November 2026
Description:
		CONFIG_ZRAM_POOL_POLICY only, mode 0400. One coherent line of named
		decimal fields in this order:
		sequence N instance N generation N enabled N initialized N backing N
		active N physical_pool_bytes N low_mib N high_mib N min_age_seconds N
		(The line above is wrapped here only.) Hysteresis asserts at >= high,
		clears at <= low. Disabled/uninitialized/no backing means inactive.
		Sequence is nonzero; changes on configuration, lifecycle or active
		state changes and on active aging retries. Counters/bytes alone do
		not advance it. Identity is the v1 STATUS instance/generation.
		Read initially, poll for POLLPRI/POLLERR, seek to zero and reread.
		sysfs_notify events coalesce: compare sequence AND lifecycle identity.
		Reads do not consume demand; they may refresh state and notify once
		if a pending transition is discovered. No queue/replay guarantee.
		Allocation/free/compaction and lifecycle events coalesce into work.
		Disabled hot paths do not queue sampling. There is no periodic idle
		poll: only while demand is active, an aging timer refreshes sequence
		every min_age_seconds (saturated to MAX_JIFFY_OFFSET ticks), allowing
		bounded backoff when no cold candidates exist. Workers do not scan
		or writeback. Removal drains both event and age work before free.

		Userspace writeback uses <linux/zram_policy.h>:
		ZRAM_IOC_WB_POLICY_EXEC = _IOWR('Z', 0x22, struct zram_wb_policy_exec)
		(0xc0e05a22), CAP_SYS_ADMIN and a writable descriptor required.
		Fixed 224 bytes native/compat: embedded v1 EXEC at offset 0, then
		aligned u64 demand_sequence@160, max_scanned_pages@168,
		min_age_ns@176, start_index@184, next_index(output)@192,
		reserved[3]@200. exec.size=224, exec.version=1 (policy version).
		mode/page_index/idle_count, all outputs and reserved must be zero.
		Nonzero request_id and demand_sequence required. Limits must satisfy
		0 < installed <= attempted <= scanned < U64_MAX. Age must be positive
		and <= S64_MAX; start_index <= U32_MAX and within initialized disk.
		Cold selection uses known boottime age >= min_age_ns, at least the
		configured age. Unknown/future timestamps are skipped. Resident
		non-SAME/non-WB/non-UNDER_WB candidates do not require IDLE or any
		cumulative idle markings. Selection is rechecked under slot lock;
		read/free/replacement invalidates pending B installs as in v1.
		At most one wrapping traversal per call; next_index is a continuation
		cursor, not a snapshot. Every visited slot counts against scanned.
		Stop reason 11 means scanned limit; installation/attempt limits take
		precedence on ties. Other outputs/error/accounting semantics are v1.
		Malformed input returns syscall errno. Accepted stale sequence or
		identity returns op_errno=-ESTALE (stop STALE); inactive demand gives
		-EAGAIN (NOT_READY); too-small age/invalid device cursor gives -EINVAL
		(ERROR). Accepted operations return syscall success with result.
		EFAULT copyout may follow effects: never blindly replay a request.
		v1 EXEC/STATUS structs, commands and capability bits are unchanged.
		A limited batch does not itself advance sequence: userspace may run
		a finite, explicitly budgeted episode using the same sequence and
		next_index while active, and must reread after stale/inactive/aging.
		No-candidate completion should wait for the next age/event sequence,
		not spin. Demand is advisory, not an automatic writeback promise.
